like, what is the difference
between Firebase and Google

00:08:17.000 --> 00:08:17.542
Cloud?

00:08:17.542 --> 00:08:19.500
And it's not so much that
there's a difference.

00:08:19.500 --> 00:08:21.680
There's sort of-- if
it's a Venn diagram,

00:08:21.680 --> 00:08:24.590
Firebase is somewhat
overlapping for the most

00:08:24.590 --> 00:08:27.080
part with Google Cloud.

00:08:27.080 --> 00:08:29.630
So they share a lot
of the same services,

00:08:29.630 --> 00:08:32.630
but the Firebase side
really is developed

00:08:32.630 --> 00:08:35.929
to be for client side
developers for the most part

00:08:35.929 --> 00:08:39.679
to make your job easier, where
Google Cloud is, generally

00:08:39.679 --> 00:08:41.223
speaking, more enterprise.

00:08:41.223 --> 00:08:43.640
MARK MIRCHANDANI: And it does
seem like Firebase is more--

00:08:43.640 --> 00:08:45.530
I've heard it referred to
as back end as a service,

00:08:45.530 --> 00:08:46.150
or maybe serverless?

00:08:46.150 --> 00:08:47.290
Would that be accurate?

00:08:47.290 --> 00:08:48.140
JEN PERSON: Yeah.

00:08:48.140 --> 00:08:49.640
So we have a whole
bunch of features

00:08:49.640 --> 00:08:51.450
that are back end as a service.

00:08:51.450 --> 00:08:53.868
We also have a lot of
growth features as well.

00:08:53.868 --> 00:08:55.910
MARK MANDEL: What do you
mean by growth features?

00:08:55.910 --> 00:08:57.200
JEN PERSON: Oh.

00:08:57.200 --> 00:08:58.560
Well, I'm glad you asked.

00:08:58.560 --> 00:09:01.010
So after your app
is built, you need

00:09:01.010 --> 00:09:05.480
a way to connect with your
users and expand that user base.

00:09:05.480 --> 00:09:08.450
So that could be
through notifications

00:09:08.450 --> 00:09:11.090
or through A/B testing.

00:09:11.090 --> 00:09:13.070
You can use Remote
Config, which allows

00:09:13.070 --> 00:09:17.730
you to change some details about
how your app works or appears.

00:09:17.730 --> 00:09:20.000
So you can try different
configurations,

00:09:20.000 --> 00:09:23.000
and see which ones
your users like better,

00:09:23.000 --> 00:09:25.130
and really just
optimize your app.

00:09:25.130 --> 00:09:27.190
And a lot of times,
we'll have a good idea,

00:09:27.190 --> 00:09:29.780
but how do you really
hone it and make it

00:09:29.780 --> 00:09:32.230
exactly what your users want?

00:09:32.230 --> 00:09:34.010
Our growth features
help you do that.

00:09:34.010 --> 00:09:35.510
MARK MIRCHANDANI:
So it's this suite

00:09:35.510 --> 00:09:37.550
of tools that help you
design all these things.

00:09:37.550 --> 00:09:40.790
And those old podcast episodes
that talk about Firebase

00:09:40.790 --> 00:09:42.570
go into a lot of details.

00:09:42.570 --> 00:09:44.683
What's new with Firebase?

00:09:44.683 --> 00:09:46.850
What have we seen, especially
with I/O just recently

00:09:46.850 --> 00:09:47.690
last month?

00:09:47.690 --> 00:09:49.470
What's new and cool with it?

00:09:49.470 --> 00:09:49.580
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:09:49.580 --> 00:09:50.538
I went back and looked.

00:09:50.538 --> 00:09:52.473
Our last episode
was back in 2017,

00:09:52.473 --> 00:09:54.140
so I assume some new
stuff has come out.

00:09:54.140 --> 00:09:55.973
MARK MIRCHANDANI:
Probably at least a thing.

00:09:55.973 --> 00:09:57.777
JEN PERSON: Yeah,
a couple things.

00:09:57.777 --> 00:10:00.110
So one of the things that's
really interesting about how

00:10:00.110 --> 00:10:03.270
Firebase has been developing
is that when we first started,

00:10:03.270 --> 00:10:05.750
we just were growing
and growing and adding

00:10:05.750 --> 00:10:07.190
all these different products.

00:10:07.190 --> 00:10:08.840
And now what I
see Firebase doing

00:10:08.840 --> 00:10:12.320
is really maturing, and adding
to those different products

00:10:12.320 --> 00:10:15.170
that we already have,
and making them really

00:10:15.170 --> 00:10:17.870
suit the developers
based on the feedback

00:10:17.870 --> 00:10:19.250
that we're getting from you.

00:10:19.250 --> 00:10:22.460
So in terms of
existing products,

00:10:22.460 --> 00:10:25.640
things we've added to
them, for Cloud Firestore,

00:10:25.640 --> 00:10:28.160
which is our second
database offering,

00:10:28.160 --> 00:10:30.710
it's also NoSQL like
the Realtime Database.

00:10:30.710 --> 00:10:33.980
And it is built of
collections of documents.

00:10:33.980 --> 00:10:35.922
It's no longer in beta.

00:10:35.922 --> 00:10:36.630
MARK MANDEL: Yay!

00:10:36.630 --> 00:10:40.495
JEN PERSON: So I know that was
a deterrent for some developers.

00:10:40.495 --> 00:10:41.870
You're worried
about, do I really

00:10:41.870 --> 00:10:44.287
want to push this to production
if it's something in beta?

00:10:44.287 --> 00:10:47.000
Well, it's not anymore, so
it's really a mature product.

00:10:47.000 --> 00:10:49.430
Really excited about
how it's been working.

00:10:49.430 --> 00:10:51.650
I absolutely love how it's
solved problems that I

00:10:51.650 --> 00:10:53.510
faced in the past with NoSQL.

00:10:53.510 --> 00:10:56.720
And very recently, we just
added collection group queries.

00:10:56.720 --> 00:11:00.500
So prior to that, you could
not make a specific query

00:11:00.500 --> 00:11:03.690
that spanned across
different subcollections

00:11:03.690 --> 00:11:05.570
and different documents.

00:11:05.570 --> 00:11:09.200
So subcollections were seen
as just completely separate

00:11:09.200 --> 00:11:10.830
entities from one another.

00:11:10.830 --> 00:11:14.780
But now you can query,
and it'll pull the key

00:11:14.780 --> 00:11:16.353
from every subcollection.

00:11:16.353 --> 00:11:17.520
MARK MIRCHANDANI: Very cool.

00:11:17.520 --> 00:11:21.290
So is this kind of like a
newer version of the Realtime

00:11:21.290 --> 00:11:22.890
Database?

00:11:22.890 --> 00:11:23.780
JEN PERSON: Yeah.

00:11:23.780 --> 00:11:26.300
I would say that that's
the simplest explanation.

00:11:26.300 --> 00:11:28.700
But we are still
definitely supporting

00:11:28.700 --> 00:11:30.050
the Realtime Database.

00:11:30.050 --> 00:11:34.220
The real time nature of it
has just been really helpful

00:11:34.220 --> 00:11:35.580
for a lot of scenarios.

00:11:35.580 --> 00:11:37.440
And so it's still
going to be there,

00:11:37.440 --> 00:11:41.490
and it still may be suited
best for certain situations.

00:11:41.490 --> 00:11:45.050
For instance, if you are
doing a whole lot of writes

00:11:45.050 --> 00:11:47.300
very quickly, the
Realtime Database

00:11:47.300 --> 00:11:50.600
might be a better solution for
you, so things like in games.

00:11:50.600 --> 00:11:54.080
But I see Cloud Firestore
as the next step

00:11:54.080 --> 00:11:57.500
because it adds stronger
querying, better querying

00:11:57.500 --> 00:12:00.410
power, a structure that
it makes more sense

00:12:00.410 --> 00:12:03.170
to mirror your
application, basically

00:12:03.170 --> 00:12:05.120
all the answers to the
questions that people

00:12:05.120 --> 00:12:06.450
had about Realtime Database.

00:12:06.450 --> 00:12:08.420
MARK MIRCHANDANI: And it has
kind of a broad use case,

00:12:08.420 --> 00:12:10.520
like you said, for a lot
of people since we were--

00:12:10.520 --> 00:12:12.895
actually, at the beginning,
just talking about, well, you

00:12:12.895 --> 00:12:15.830
can actually get
Firestore through GCP

00:12:15.830 --> 00:12:18.310
rather than through-- you can
do it through both Firebase

00:12:18.310 --> 00:12:21.207
or through GCP, again,
depending on your use case.

00:12:21.207 --> 00:12:22.290
JEN PERSON: Yeah, exactly.

00:12:22.290 --> 00:12:24.500
And that actually
means that if you're

00:12:24.500 --> 00:12:26.785
starting with a very
small team, and you're

00:12:26.785 --> 00:12:28.160
building an
application, and then

00:12:28.160 --> 00:12:31.910
it becomes extremely popular,
switching over to Google Cloud

00:12:31.910 --> 00:12:32.410
is a breeze.

00:12:32.410 --> 00:12:33.910
MARK MANDEL: Actually,
I don't think

00:12:33.910 --> 00:12:35.780
we've ever actually
talked about that on any

00:12:35.780 --> 00:12:37.100
of the previous episodes.

00:12:37.100 --> 00:12:39.050
Because there's a really
nice graduation path,

00:12:39.050 --> 00:12:40.790
I think, as you go
to Firebase, and then

00:12:40.790 --> 00:12:42.668
if you wanted to add
extra features into GCP.

00:12:42.668 --> 00:12:44.210
Can you talk a little
bit about that?

00:12:44.210 --> 00:12:45.168
JEN PERSON: Yeah, sure.

00:12:45.168 --> 00:12:47.780
So whenever you create
a Firebase project,

00:12:47.780 --> 00:12:50.060
it's also a Google
Cloud project which

00:12:50.060 --> 00:12:54.200
enables you to implement any
of the Google Cloud APIs right

00:12:54.200 --> 00:12:57.050
from the Google Cloud console.

00:12:57.050 --> 00:12:59.060
And there is also,
as you pointed out,

00:12:59.060 --> 00:13:02.630
some overlapping features
like Cloud Firestore,

00:13:02.630 --> 00:13:06.020
like Cloud Storage, which
is available specifically

00:13:06.020 --> 00:13:07.700
for Firebase, but
it's the same Cloud

00:13:07.700 --> 00:13:10.370
Storage underneath that
we use in Google Cloud.

00:13:10.370 --> 00:13:13.800
And recently, we added
Identity Platform.

00:13:13.800 --> 00:13:17.480
So now we have auth
features in cloud

00:13:17.480 --> 00:13:20.660
that add on to the
Firebase Auth features

00:13:20.660 --> 00:13:24.002
and really are suited for
those enterprise situations.

00:13:24.002 --> 00:13:25.460
And switching over
is really simple

00:13:25.460 --> 00:13:27.560
because you already
have your project.

00:13:27.560 --> 00:13:29.330
You don't have to
worry about trying

00:13:29.330 --> 00:13:30.732
to link projects together.

00:13:30.732 --> 00:13:32.690
MARK MIRCHANDANI: So it
might be a good example

00:13:32.690 --> 00:13:34.527
that someone started
with Firebase,

00:13:34.527 --> 00:13:36.110
and then they added
a little bit more,

00:13:36.110 --> 00:13:38.990
and then they wanted to build
in some more GCP projects--

00:13:38.990 --> 00:13:40.033
or GCP products.

00:13:40.033 --> 00:13:41.450
But it might also
go the other way

00:13:41.450 --> 00:13:43.310
where you're using
GCP products, and you

00:13:43.310 --> 00:13:45.590
want to add a mobile app
or something like that.

00:13:45.590 --> 00:13:47.090
And it would make
just as much sense

00:13:47.090 --> 00:13:49.662
to create that Firebase project
and go off of that, right?

00:13:49.662 --> 00:13:50.870
JEN PERSON: Yeah, absolutely.

00:13:50.870 --> 00:13:52.912
And if you have an existing
Google Cloud project,

00:13:52.912 --> 00:13:55.730
you can import it into Firebase.

00:13:55.730 --> 00:13:58.220
So yeah, you can
definitely go either way.

00:13:58.220 --> 00:14:00.690
And there are situations
where, as you pointed out,

00:14:00.690 --> 00:14:03.450
if you want to build a client
app, that would make sense.

00:14:03.450 --> 00:14:08.660
And a lot of times, we will
be using cloud APIs even when

00:14:08.660 --> 00:14:09.740
you're using Firebase.

00:14:09.740 --> 00:14:12.240
So if you wanted to do some
sort of translation server

00:14:12.240 --> 00:14:14.090
side or something
like that, we would

00:14:14.090 --> 00:14:15.202
use Google Cloud for that.

00:14:15.202 --> 00:14:16.160
MARK MANDEL: Very cool.

00:14:16.160 --> 00:14:18.770
You mentioned one thing
in passing that is--

00:14:18.770 --> 00:14:19.440
I don't know.

00:14:19.440 --> 00:14:21.440
It's always been my
favorite feature of Firebase

00:14:21.440 --> 00:14:22.670
is Firebase Auth.

00:14:22.670 --> 00:14:24.170
And having built--

00:14:24.170 --> 00:14:26.090
I don't know--
websites for 20 years,

00:14:26.090 --> 00:14:28.220
not having to build an
authentication scheme

00:14:28.220 --> 00:14:29.030
seems really cool.

00:14:29.030 --> 00:14:30.200
But it looks like you've
had some improvements

00:14:30.200 --> 00:14:31.760
in the last couple
of years on that too.

00:14:31.760 --> 00:14:32.468
JEN PERSON: Yeah.

00:14:32.468 --> 00:14:34.910
In the last couple of years,
we've added several new ways

00:14:34.910 --> 00:14:36.110
to authenticate.

00:14:36.110 --> 00:14:37.370
We added phone auth.

00:14:37.370 --> 00:14:39.000
We added email link auth.

00:14:39.000 --> 00:14:41.120
So rather than having
an email and password,

00:14:41.120 --> 00:14:43.070
you can send an email link.

00:14:43.070 --> 00:14:45.440
And you can click
on it in your email.

00:14:45.440 --> 00:14:48.150
We've added different
email actions.

00:14:48.150 --> 00:14:50.973
So in the console,
you can decide

00:14:50.973 --> 00:14:53.390
what is the message that you
want your clients to get when

00:14:53.390 --> 00:14:56.300
they get, say, an
email to verify,

00:14:56.300 --> 00:14:59.090
or you can change that
from the client as well.

00:14:59.090 --> 00:15:02.840
And our latest identity
providers that we've added

00:15:02.840 --> 00:15:05.300
are Microsoft and Yahoo Auth.

00:15:05.300 --> 00:15:08.930
And now we're going to be using
a generic OAuth 2 provider

00:15:08.930 --> 00:15:09.740
sign-in.

00:15:09.740 --> 00:15:12.770
So going forward, it's
going to be even easier

00:15:12.770 --> 00:15:15.050
to add different providers.

00:15:15.050 --> 00:15:17.990
You may have noticed in the
past each provider maybe

00:15:17.990 --> 00:15:20.960
has a little bit different
way that it works.

00:15:20.960 --> 00:15:23.540
You need to log in,
say, with Facebook,

00:15:23.540 --> 00:15:25.490
and then they have
their own portal,

00:15:25.490 --> 00:15:27.530
and then you have to
take that information

00:15:27.530 --> 00:15:29.870
and use it to log
in to Firebase.

00:15:29.870 --> 00:15:33.590
But now with that OAuth 2
provider sign-in being generic,

00:15:33.590 --> 00:15:36.350
it basically just
pops up a web window,

00:15:36.350 --> 00:15:37.640
and they all work the same.

00:15:37.640 --> 00:15:39.740
And that's going to make
it a lot easier for us

00:15:39.740 --> 00:15:41.480
too to add more providers.

00:15:41.480 --> 00:15:44.570
So hopefully we'll have
more providers going forward

00:15:44.570 --> 00:15:45.230
quicker.

00:15:45.230 --> 00:15:46.790
MARK MANDEL: No more having
to implement and reset

00:15:46.790 --> 00:15:47.930
your password functionality.

00:15:47.930 --> 00:15:49.070
MARK MIRCHANDANI: Don't
build your own auth.

00:15:49.070 --> 00:15:49.685
Just don't.

00:15:49.685 --> 00:15:50.120
MARK MANDEL: Don't

00:15:50.120 --> 00:15:51.500
MARK MIRCHANDANI: There's a
very small set of use cases

00:15:51.500 --> 00:15:52.470
where you need to.

00:15:52.470 --> 00:15:55.040
But otherwise, man,
you just don't.

00:15:55.040 --> 00:15:56.120
JEN PERSON: Yeah.

00:15:56.120 --> 00:16:00.230
One time I watched this how
it's made show on YouTube

00:16:00.230 --> 00:16:02.150
about making soy sauce.

00:16:02.150 --> 00:16:06.290
And soy sauce is basically
water and salt and soy beans.

00:16:06.290 --> 00:16:07.820
Right?

00:16:07.820 --> 00:16:10.880
So you could theoretically
make your own soy sauce.

00:16:10.880 --> 00:16:12.860
But wouldn't you rather
just make dinner?

00:16:12.860 --> 00:16:14.390
That's kind of how
I think about it.

00:16:14.390 --> 00:16:16.348
Yes, you can spend all
of your development time

00:16:16.348 --> 00:16:20.330
making this very complex
thing like handling auth,

00:16:20.330 --> 00:16:22.760
or you can make what
you went out to make,

00:16:22.760 --> 00:16:25.460
the application that you
wanted to make, and leave that

00:16:25.460 --> 00:16:28.015
to us because we have all
this experience with it.

00:16:28.015 --> 00:16:29.390
MARK MIRCHANDANI:
I feel that way

00:16:29.390 --> 00:16:31.970
about any cooking whatsoever.

00:16:31.970 --> 00:16:33.620
I could make this
bowl of cereal,

00:16:33.620 --> 00:16:36.065
but wouldn't I rather have
someone make the bowl of cereal

00:16:36.065 --> 00:16:37.060
and just give it to me?

00:16:37.060 --> 00:16:38.380
MARK MANDEL: And just
deliver it to my house.

00:16:38.380 --> 00:16:39.338
MARK MIRCHANDANI: Yeah.

00:16:39.338 --> 00:16:41.700
JEN PERSON: That's a
great idea for an app--

00:16:41.700 --> 00:16:42.393
cereal delivery.

00:16:42.393 --> 00:16:44.060
MARK MANDEL: I would
pay money for that.

00:16:44.060 --> 00:16:46.440
MARK MIRCHANDANI: I, sadly,
would pay money for that.

00:16:46.440 --> 00:16:47.030
MARK MANDEL: Excellent.

00:16:47.030 --> 00:16:48.410
One thing I actually
have been also dying

00:16:48.410 --> 00:16:50.420
to talk about on the
podcast, but we haven't had--

00:16:50.420 --> 00:16:52.170
just haven't had a
chance, unfortunately--

00:16:52.170 --> 00:16:54.230
ML Kit is super interesting.

00:16:54.230 --> 00:16:55.230
Tell us more about that.

00:16:55.230 --> 00:16:56.110
I think that's fascinating.

00:16:56.110 --> 00:16:56.818
JEN PERSON: Yeah.

00:16:56.818 --> 00:17:00.950
So what ML Kit does is it
provides you a simplified way

00:17:00.950 --> 00:17:07.250
to use machine learning on your
client's app or on the server.

00:17:07.250 --> 00:17:10.579
And we have all sorts
of different ML features

00:17:10.579 --> 00:17:12.470
that we're slowly adding to it.

00:17:12.470 --> 00:17:16.550
And so the benefit of using
ML Kit is that we have--

00:17:16.550 --> 00:17:19.670
with on-device ML
features, you are

00:17:19.670 --> 00:17:23.250
able to use them even if you
don't have internet access.

00:17:23.250 --> 00:17:25.880
So it leads to a much
quicker experience.

00:17:25.880 --> 00:17:29.300
We're able to do things
like if you have a video,

00:17:29.300 --> 00:17:31.280
and you're just
filming in the moment,

00:17:31.280 --> 00:17:34.790
you could do real time
text recognition or face

00:17:34.790 --> 00:17:37.500
detection, because it's
right there on the device.

00:17:37.500 --> 00:17:38.988
So it's super fast.

00:17:38.988 --> 00:17:41.030
And if you need something
a little more powerful,

00:17:41.030 --> 00:17:43.070
you could still
use the cloud side,

00:17:43.070 --> 00:17:45.910
and it's done simply
through our SDK.

00:17:45.910 --> 00:17:49.700
So right now for ML Kit,
wow, it's growing so fast.

00:17:49.700 --> 00:17:51.710
I swear there's a new
feature every week.

00:17:51.710 --> 00:17:54.320
I actually had to look it up
to make sure I got them all.

00:17:54.320 --> 00:17:57.500
In terms of natural
language, we have

00:17:57.500 --> 00:17:59.420
identifying language of text.

00:17:59.420 --> 00:18:02.450
We have Translate, and we
also added Smart Reply.

00:18:02.450 --> 00:18:04.700
We have a lot of
Cloud Vision features,

00:18:04.700 --> 00:18:08.570
including recognizing text,
detecting faces, including

00:18:08.570 --> 00:18:10.370
landmarks on the
face-- so not just

00:18:10.370 --> 00:18:13.490
detecting the square of a
face, but where's the nose,

00:18:13.490 --> 00:18:14.330
where are the eyes?

00:18:14.330 --> 00:18:16.970
It helps you make all
those fun filters.

00:18:16.970 --> 00:18:19.820
Scanning barcodes,
which is super popular,

00:18:19.820 --> 00:18:22.040
labeling images,
detecting and tracking

00:18:22.040 --> 00:18:23.570
objects, which we
recently added,

00:18:23.570 --> 00:18:26.240
and recognizing
popular landmarks.

00:18:26.240 --> 00:18:29.540
And we also added
AutoML Vision Edge.

00:18:29.540 --> 00:18:32.420
So if you have a
custom AutoML model,

00:18:32.420 --> 00:18:34.615
you can access it
right on your device.

00:18:34.615 --> 00:18:35.990
So you can see
that's another way

00:18:35.990 --> 00:18:39.980
that there's an overlap between
Google Cloud and Firebase.

00:18:39.980 --> 00:18:42.470
And if you really need
a customized model,

00:18:42.470 --> 00:18:44.090
you can use TensorFlow Lite.

00:18:44.090 --> 00:18:47.708
You can run your TensorFlow
Lite model through ML Kit.

00:18:47.708 --> 00:18:50.000
MARK MIRCHANDANI: So it's
pretty much ML Kit is the way

00:18:50.000 --> 00:18:53.970
to use any ML technology
with your mobile slash web

00:18:53.970 --> 00:18:55.410
slash whatever app, right?

00:18:55.410 --> 00:18:57.410
JEN PERSON: With your iOS
and Android app, yeah.

00:18:57.410 --> 00:19:01.680
Our goal, I think, is just to
cover any and all scenarios.

00:19:01.680 --> 00:19:03.650
And we're really excited
to see how people

00:19:03.650 --> 00:19:05.160
