00:20:25.222 --> 00:20:26.200
seems like a good idea.

00:20:26.200 --> 00:20:27.230
MARK MANDEL: Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:20:27.230 --> 00:20:27.620
Just guess.

00:20:27.620 --> 00:20:28.220
It'll be fine.

00:20:28.220 --> 00:20:28.880
It'll be fine.

00:20:28.880 --> 00:20:29.588
JEN PERSON: Yeah.

00:20:29.588 --> 00:20:30.350
So let's see.

00:20:30.350 --> 00:20:32.562
We have now a
Crashlytics trigger.

00:20:32.562 --> 00:20:33.770
MARK MANDEL: Oh, that's cool.

00:20:33.770 --> 00:20:34.880
JEN PERSON: So if you
have some sort of crash,

00:20:34.880 --> 00:20:37.070
you can trigger a
function, and then you

00:20:37.070 --> 00:20:40.340
can use that function to, say,
send a notification to Slack

00:20:40.340 --> 00:20:41.780
or something like that.

00:20:41.780 --> 00:20:46.700
And my favorite-- we now have
scheduled functions, so big

00:20:46.700 --> 00:20:47.330
request there.

00:20:47.330 --> 00:20:48.950
You can now schedule
your functions

00:20:48.950 --> 00:20:52.220
to run at a specific
time and repeat.

00:20:52.220 --> 00:20:56.450
And we have a new emulator that
can run your functions right

00:20:56.450 --> 00:20:59.270
on your device without
touching your live code.

00:20:59.270 --> 00:21:01.970
And it also emulates all
sorts of different things

00:21:01.970 --> 00:21:02.960
in your app.

00:21:02.960 --> 00:21:04.670
If you're using
Cloud Firestore, you

00:21:04.670 --> 00:21:08.150
can basically have a whole
ecosystem working right

00:21:08.150 --> 00:21:10.970
from your device and not
worry about accessing

00:21:10.970 --> 00:21:12.430
external live code.

00:21:12.430 --> 00:21:13.430
And you can test it out.

00:21:13.430 --> 00:21:14.347
MARK MANDEL: Oh, nice.

00:21:14.347 --> 00:21:16.520
Is that using the Firebase
command line utility

00:21:16.520 --> 00:21:17.730
or something to set that up?

00:21:17.730 --> 00:21:19.815
JEN PERSON: Yeah, the
command line utility.

00:21:19.815 --> 00:21:21.690
MARK MANDEL: Well, that
was way easier than--

00:21:21.690 --> 00:21:22.700
MARK MIRCHANDANI: I thought
there was going to be more.

00:21:22.700 --> 00:21:23.510
MARK MANDEL: I thought
there was going to be more.

00:21:23.510 --> 00:21:24.350
It's like, no. no.

00:21:24.350 --> 00:21:25.230
You just go Firebase.

00:21:25.230 --> 00:21:26.090
JEN PERSON: Yeah.

00:21:26.090 --> 00:21:28.460
When you have the
CLI, you just have it.

00:21:28.460 --> 00:21:30.710
MARK MIRCHANDANI: So a common
use case for some people

00:21:30.710 --> 00:21:32.293
might be setting up
their app, hooking

00:21:32.293 --> 00:21:34.230
into a Firestore
for the database.

00:21:34.230 --> 00:21:35.957
And then when some
event happens,

00:21:35.957 --> 00:21:37.790
or when they upload a
certain amount of data

00:21:37.790 --> 00:21:40.467
to Firestore, that then
triggers a function,

00:21:40.467 --> 00:21:42.050
does a little bit
of processing on it,

00:21:42.050 --> 00:21:44.092
and then maybe puts it
back in, or like you said,

00:21:44.092 --> 00:21:45.150
sends a notification.

00:21:45.150 --> 00:21:47.257
The possibilities are
probably endless there.

00:21:47.257 --> 00:21:48.590
JEN PERSON: Oh, yes, definitely.

00:21:48.590 --> 00:21:50.510
And it's great because
it also connects

00:21:50.510 --> 00:21:52.950
with all sorts of
third-party services as well.

00:21:52.950 --> 00:21:55.850
So it's not just exclusively
within the Google Cloud

00:21:55.850 --> 00:21:56.420
environment.

00:21:56.420 --> 00:21:59.360
You're able to incorporate
whatever third-party services

00:21:59.360 --> 00:21:59.880
you need.

00:21:59.880 --> 00:22:01.760
And for now, until we
add different types

00:22:01.760 --> 00:22:03.770
