00:22:19.790 --> 00:22:23.130
if you want some ideas about
how you could use them.

00:22:23.130 --> 00:22:23.880
MARK MANDEL: Cool.

00:22:23.880 --> 00:22:25.713
We can put a link to
that in the show notes.

00:22:25.713 --> 00:22:29.060
I'd love to know what's been
the most interesting or strange

00:22:29.060 --> 00:22:31.800
thing you've seen somebody
build with Firebase.

00:22:31.800 --> 00:22:34.010
JEN PERSON: That's
a great question.

00:22:34.010 --> 00:22:34.880
Oh, OK.

00:22:34.880 --> 00:22:38.540
So when I was at a hackathon--
so you're looking at having

00:22:38.540 --> 00:22:40.790
a really limited
amount of time--

00:22:40.790 --> 00:22:43.890
right now, in terms of deploying
Cloud Functions to Firebase,

00:22:43.890 --> 00:22:45.890
you do that from the
command line interface.

00:22:45.890 --> 00:22:48.140
But what they did is
basically make a website

00:22:48.140 --> 00:22:51.140
where you can just type your
JavaScript code right into it,

00:22:51.140 --> 00:22:53.130
and it deploys it
automatically for you.

00:22:53.130 --> 00:22:55.790
And the way that it
worked was actually

00:22:55.790 --> 00:22:58.460
just taking the actual
chunks of your code,

00:22:58.460 --> 00:23:00.800
and turning them into
strings, and putting them

00:23:00.800 --> 00:23:02.120
into the database.

00:23:02.120 --> 00:23:04.130
And that would
trigger your function

00:23:04.130 --> 00:23:06.830
that then reassembled
it and made that

00:23:06.830 --> 00:23:08.600
into a Cloud Function.

00:23:08.600 --> 00:23:11.150
I just thought it was a really
fascinating implementation.

00:23:11.150 --> 00:23:13.400
MARK MIRCHANDANI: There's
no way that could be abused.

00:23:13.400 --> 00:23:15.250
Not a single-- not a single way.

00:23:15.250 --> 00:23:17.000
MARK MANDEL: Oh, it's
super secure, super.

00:23:17.000 --> 00:23:17.640
MARK MIRCHANDANI: Absolutely.

00:23:17.640 --> 00:23:19.880
Just give the ability for
anyone to put any code anywhere.

00:23:19.880 --> 00:23:20.150
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:23:20.150 --> 00:23:22.250
JEN PERSON: That's why it's
a hackathon project, not

00:23:22.250 --> 00:23:22.790
a real one.

00:23:22.790 --> 00:23:23.350
But--

00:23:23.350 --> 00:23:24.650
MARK MANDEL: You don't have
that project handy, do you?

00:23:24.650 --> 00:23:25.502
I mean, never mind.

00:23:25.502 --> 00:23:26.210
JEN PERSON: Yeah.

00:23:26.210 --> 00:23:27.650
So I thought that
was pretty cool.

00:23:27.650 --> 00:23:29.150
MARK MIRCHANDANI:
That is very cool.

