00:31:52.430 --> 00:31:54.710
What I want to know
is, how do I save money

00:31:54.710 --> 00:31:57.170
on my Google Cloud
Platform resources,

00:31:57.170 --> 00:31:58.315
just across all of them?

00:31:58.315 --> 00:31:59.690
Can you solve all
those problems?

00:31:59.690 --> 00:32:01.040
MARK MIRCHANDANI: Just
every, every resource?

00:32:01.040 --> 00:32:03.490
Well, the first thing you do
is just shut them all down.

00:32:03.490 --> 00:32:05.240
MARK MANDEL: That is a good
way of saving money, yes.

00:32:05.240 --> 00:32:07.115
MARK MIRCHANDANI: You
will not spend anything

00:32:07.115 --> 00:32:08.260
if you don't use anything.

00:32:08.260 --> 00:32:08.760
Right?

00:32:08.760 --> 00:32:10.310
The nice advantage of the cloud.

00:32:10.310 --> 00:32:13.020
Turns out that does not
work for most people.

00:32:13.020 --> 00:32:15.530
So a more realistic
approach is to actually try

00:32:15.530 --> 00:32:17.210
to figure out what
you have that's

00:32:17.210 --> 00:32:19.418
up and running that you're
paying for that you're not

00:32:19.418 --> 00:32:20.150
really using.

00:32:20.150 --> 00:32:22.025
And so there was this
super cool talk at Next

00:32:22.025 --> 00:32:24.525
that's on YouTube-- and we'll
have a link in the description

00:32:24.525 --> 00:32:25.040
below--

00:32:25.040 --> 00:32:27.050
that talks a little
bit about how

00:32:27.050 --> 00:32:30.380
to automate cost optimization
toolings basically

00:32:30.380 --> 00:32:32.150
by scanning your
resources for things

00:32:32.150 --> 00:32:33.830
you're not using that
you are paying for

00:32:33.830 --> 00:32:35.210
and then shutting them down.

00:32:35.210 --> 00:32:37.710
You can write that using Cloud
Functions pretty quickly,

00:32:37.710 --> 00:32:38.960
and then it just goes into it.

00:32:38.960 --> 00:32:41.168
So in the talk, they talk
a little bit about-- what's

00:32:41.168 --> 00:32:42.140
the example they give?

00:32:42.140 --> 00:32:43.967
Looking at-- so
external IP addresses--

00:32:43.967 --> 00:32:46.550
I don't know if you knew this,
but you don't pay for them when

00:32:46.550 --> 00:32:47.750
they're attached to a resource.

00:32:47.750 --> 00:32:48.980
MARK MANDEL: But you do pay
for them if they're not.

00:32:48.980 --> 00:32:49.890
MARK MIRCHANDANI: There it is.

00:32:49.890 --> 00:32:51.800
And so if you have an
external IP address that's

00:32:51.800 --> 00:32:54.140
been sitting around for 30 days,
that's a pretty good chance

00:32:54.140 --> 00:32:55.010
you may not need it.

00:32:55.010 --> 00:32:56.360
Of course, that will
[? be ?] up to your business

00:32:56.360 --> 00:32:58.110
logic if you actually
want it or not.

00:32:58.110 --> 00:33:00.412
But if you're not saving
those for anything,

00:33:00.412 --> 00:33:02.120
you can actually write
this cool function

00:33:02.120 --> 00:33:03.867
that would go scan
all your IP addresses,

00:33:03.867 --> 00:33:05.700
see which ones haven't
been used in a while,

00:33:05.700 --> 00:33:09.440
and then shut them off, put that
on a Cloud Scheduler job maybe.

00:33:09.440 --> 00:33:11.510
And then you've got
this great system

00:33:11.510 --> 00:33:13.525
to automatically scan
for it and delete it,

00:33:13.525 --> 00:33:14.900
saving you a little
bit of money.

00:33:14.900 --> 00:33:16.460
So you can do that with
external IP addresses.

00:33:16.460 --> 00:33:19.070
The talk also shows doing that
with persistent disks that

00:33:19.070 --> 00:33:21.802
haven't been used, any
resources, Compute Engine.

00:33:21.802 --> 00:33:23.510
MARK MANDEL: Load
balancers that might be

00:33:23.510 --> 00:33:24.980
